+# Agent execution reliability telemetry (2026-10-18)
+
+## Change
+
+The Agent evaluation API now returns `execution_groups` independently of operator feedback. The summary covers the most recent 200 operations returned by the existing operation store and groups by mode, model, prompt version, and tool policy version. It includes persisted task states, result count, structured-answer validation (accepted/rejected/unknown), repair operation/attempt counts, recorded tool outcomes, skipped model-requested tool calls, and whether provider-reported token usage is complete.
+
+The existing `groups` and `paired_comparisons` remain human-feedback-only. The new fields explicitly state `execution_accuracy_claim: false` and explain that these workflow metrics cannot establish semantic citation support, vulnerability accuracy, precision, recall, or ground truth. Operations without results contribute status and unknown validation/usage counts instead of disappearing.
+
+The Agent operations panel displays this as a separate reliability card, distinct from human-feedback evaluation. It does not display provider secrets, source, raw model output, or operation error text.
+
+## Verification
+
+- `PYTHONPATH=.:tests /home/audit/ohscanner-workbench/venv/bin/python -m unittest test_agent_workflow -v`: 100 tests passed.
+- Web tests: 35 passed. The production bundle contains the new card and was served by the live panel. A direct TypeScript check via Windows Node over the WSL filesystem did not terminate cleanly, so a clean typecheck result is not claimed.
+- Live panel restarted on `http://127.0.0.1:18779/` after verifying no queued or running Agent operations. The preserved data directory is `/home/audit/ohscanner/records/agent-llm-20261018/live-smoke-data`; provider status remains configured.
+- Live `/api/v1/agent/evaluation-summary` responds HTTP 200 with `execution_groups`, and the existing feedback accuracy claim remains false.
+- No LLM/provider request, source egress, candidate status change, push, PR, or merge was performed.
+
+## Limits
+
+These counts describe the bounded 200-operation listing, not all historical operations. Missing result telemetry is shown as unknown. A schema-accepted answer only passed the server's structural/source-binding validation; semantic claim support remains unverified. Tool counts report recorded statuses and are not success or security judgments.
+
+## Follow-up: retain model attribution for failed tasks
+
+The operation spec already snapshots the public `provider_model` at enqueue time, but the telemetry initially looked for a different key. Failed tasks therefore appeared as `model=unknown` even though their immutable spec carried the model. The summary now reads that existing snapshot as its fallback. A regression test uses the actual queued-spec field.
+
+Verification: Agent workflow, workbench, and owner integration suites passed 147 tests. After a guarded restart with no running or executable queued operations, the live API grouped all 9 failed runs under `deepseek-v4-pro` and reduced unknown-model operations from 9 to 0. Three queued `candidate_review_batch` records remained untouched; the operation worker explicitly excludes these batches. Provider configuration stayed enabled, the panel API returned HTTP 200, and `accuracy_claim` remained false. No model request was made.
+
+## Follow-up: per-claim semantic support review
+
+Normal Agent operation details now let a human label each model claim's cited evidence as `supported`, `partially_supported`, `unsupported`, or `unverifiable`. Submission requires one unique adjudication for every claim; a review with no adjudications is retained as `not_reviewed`. The feedback summary reports these labels separately from usefulness and citation correctness and explicitly does not treat them as vulnerability truth or accuracy.
+
+Verification on 2026-10-18: Agent workflow, workbench, and owner integration suites passed 147 tests, including full-coverage validation, aggregate counts, and legacy `not_reviewed` behavior. Web tests passed 35/35; TypeScript `tsc --noEmit` and the production bundle build passed with Node 24.16.0; `git diff --check` passed. The live 18779 panel served the refreshed bundle, showed the updated semantic-review boundary, and retained the fixed candidate as `candidate` with its evidence obligations `unknown`. No operator review was submitted, no model/source request was made, and no R0 or paired baseline was fabricated. Provider-call behavior and vulnerability accuracy are not established by these UI/workflow tests.
+
+## Follow-up: explain incomplete provider responses in the operation detail
+
+A real 18779 record ca9a72878dab4be29aee25b3b3558030 has structured_answer_validation=provider_incomplete and structured_answer_validation_error_code=max_output_tokens; the server discarded its partial response and left all five evidence obligations unresolved. The Agent detail component previously showed its generic result limitations but omitted the structured rejection alert because the UI helper accepted only rejected_* statuses. The UI now renders an explicit output-limit message, preserves the unresolved/candidate boundary, and tells the operator that any retry needs a fresh preview and separate authorization. Other incomplete Provider responses receive a generic fail-closed message.
+
+Verification: two regression tests cover output-limit and other incomplete responses; Web tests pass 37/37, TypeScript check and production bundle build pass. The refreshed 18779 browser page for the recorded operation visibly showed the output-token-limit alert, candidate status, and five unresolved obligations. No review was submitted and no Provider request was made. This improves operator interpretation and retry safety; it does not repair the old operation or establish Agent accuracy.
+
+## Follow-up: recover from unsupported model tool queries
+
+Read-only review of the nine failed Agent worker logs found several distinct fail-closed causes: conservative input-request budget exhaustion, read-only tool-call budget exhaustion, a queued policy/version mismatch, an incomplete Provider response, and a rejected authorization-policy answer followed by input-budget exhaustion. One older task exited when the model requested symbol lookup with unsupported characters. The current tool loop already returns ValueError query rejections to the model; this follow-up gives invalid symbol queries their own stable error code and fixed permitted-character guidance, so the model can correct or stop without exposing the raw rejected text in the error result. The stored tool arguments still follow the existing sensitive-pattern redaction policy, and audit events retain only their argument hash.
+
+Verification: a Fake Provider integration test now exercises both an oversized source range and a token-shaped invalid symbol query in one bounded investigation. The first returns source_range_too_large; the second returns invalid_symbol_query with safe guidance, and the answer remains unknown/candidate with the evidence obligation unresolved. The token-shaped query is redacted from the persisted argument trace, while the event contains only the argument hash. Agent workflow, workbench, and owner integration tests passed 148 tests. After confirming that only the three non-executable candidate_review_batch records were queued, the 18779 service was restarted from this worktree; /api/v1/health returned ok, the Worker is running, and the target roots match the isolated configuration. No Provider request or queued batch execution occurred.
