+# CVE-2026-46675 known-positive scan experiment — 2026-10-18
+
+## Purpose and evidence labels
+
+This two-part pilot uses the first public disclosure in `OHScanner_G盘源码漏洞审查记录_20261018.md` as its external R0 known-positive reference for the libpng component. Its R0 scope is component status only (`confirmed`); OpenHarmony product reachability remains `unknown`. This external disclosure is not a panel-stored human static baseline, and the separate DSoftBus R2 smoke below is not a matched comparison. The supplied review labels the libpng 1.6.55 component path `confirmed` and OpenHarmony product reachability `unknown`; it also says the prior ASan result is isolated Harness evidence, not a product crash. This run preserves those labels.
+
+## Fixed source and scan
+
+The G-drive packaging repository is pinned at commit `e57b1079564a02a50321ec528aa6e41884979d32`, tree `02b17492f4490ff2e4d17ba74e2080358a500059`. A WSL status check currently reports many tracked files as modified in that mounted checkout, so the experiment did not use its working-tree contents and did not change it. The scan used an isolated source fixture extracted from the committed `libpng-1.6.55.tar.gz` archive (SHA-256 `71a2c5b1218f60c4c6d2f1954c7eb20132156cae90bdb90b566c24db002782a6`). The fixture commit `eb90bcf1948cda4d68580351ed8cc71195965a46` / tree `a6ab8825af527ee4be91e5d8625993d86c5dbe32` is a local source-binding wrapper, not an upstream commit. Fixture snapshot SHA-256: `339553dcd9bd3c670054c2c76fd26a1649ce74e2d3ee210bbed7c7302401cb30`.
+
+OHScanner job `072fdf62159243509da8d496da21f45d` completed with limitations; report SHA-256 `0ad645e4157c5ba7b15280e20fc2e731634e68fa44fd7c99076e020d9a120602`; plan SHA-256 `9cc0914bc130727ae1dbae38e440fc149d4e0c4a842ad31d60b2b663f1a1e483`. Selected detectors: `ohscanner.cpp.bounds` 0.2.0, `ohscanner.cpp.integer_bounds` 0.2.0, and `ohscanner.cpp.unsafe_memory_call` 0.1.0. The scan inventoried 98 C/C++ files (37 complete, 61 partial) and emitted 152 candidates. It emitted no candidate in `pngread.c` or `pngrutil.c`; its three candidates in `pngdebug.h` do not map to this CVE. The report records `validated_vulnerability=0`.
+
+## Agent workflow decision
+
+For the first shippable Agent path, choose **R2 bounded read-only tool-agent**. Pure LLM is cheaper and faster, but it sees only the initial excerpt and gives weaker source-verification against unsupported claims. R1 is a useful one-shot baseline, but cannot follow a claim into another bounded source range. R2 keeps repository, commit, file and line references fixed, lets the model request only approved read-only source tools, validates the returned schema/citations, and leaves unresolved obligations unknown. Selecting only R2 now keeps implementation, user consent, and the first experiment easy to review; R1 and pure LLM remain later comparison arms when there is a matched R0 and enough samples. This is a workflow tradeoff, not a claim that R2 is more accurate.
+
+The R2 provider call was **not made** for CVE-2026-46675: no scanner candidate maps to the disclosed path, while the panel requires every Agent operation and static R0 to bind to a completed scan candidate. The current evaluation endpoint has zero R0 baselines. The public disclosure is therefore recorded as an external known-positive reference, not as a source-matched human R0 for an unrelated finding. No DeepSeek request was sent in this experiment.
+
+## Conclusion and next gate
+
+This run shows that the selected detector set did not produce a CVE-matched candidate in the relevant libpng implementation files under a partially parsed scan. It is not a whole-scanner false-negative or recall estimate, an Agent result, or product vulnerability confirmation. Component status remains `confirmed`; OpenHarmony product reachability remains `unknown`; Agent assessment remains `unknown`.
+
+The practical next step is to add a small source-bound reference-sample entry that allows a human to record R0 when a known-positive disclosure has no scanner finding, then run the selected R2 workflow on that exact fixed source. Until that entry exists, attaching this CVE to an unrelated scanner candidate would create invalid evaluation data.
+
+## Separate R2 integration smoke (not CVE-matched)
+
+To verify that the selected workflow executes end to end, one independent R2 smoke was run against the already selected DSoftBus candidate, not against libpng: operation `3750f6e793b64d56905c29f4cc4c66ee`, candidate `533e39744fcd38b365d4e97431dc2d2c46d36301cf6435c916e9a22547033162`, fixed commit `9f922677e147b939d6db899bde181d609d64711b`, report SHA-256 `fd007353b07f1a28c0650cc588c03aa89154ecdb25368beb53364489ae7c5e18`. This was standalone with no experiment ID because the service correctly refused a paired experiment without a same-candidate R0.
+
+The DeepSeek v4 Pro operation completed with limitations: 4 provider calls (20,086 input and 1,070 output tokens), 4 bounded read-only tool calls, one invalid structured response followed by one bounded repair accepted by server validation. Agent assessment was `needs_more_evidence`; zero supported claims; all five obligations remained unresolved; vulnerability state stayed `candidate` and `validated_vulnerability=0`. The model explicitly left dispatch authorization, caller reachability, exported capability impact, and parser limitations unresolved. This demonstrates that the R2 path, fixed-source tools, schema rejection, bounded repair and conservative fallback work together. It is only an operational smoke; it is not paired with an R0 and does not measure accuracy or confirm/refute the DSoftBus candidate.
+
+
+## Additional public-CVE candidate probe: CVE-2025-57052
+
+To find a disclosed sample that the current candidate rules could hand to R2, the upstream vulnerable parent of the official cJSON fix was checked. GitHub identifies commit `74e1ff4994aa4139126967f6d289b675b4b36fef` as fixing CVE-2025-57052 and gives parent `8f2beb57ddad1f94bed899790b00f46df893ccac`; the one-line fix changes the loop condition from `pointer[0] <= '9'` to `pointer[position] <= '9'` in `cJSON_Utils.c:285` ([upstream fix](https://github.com/DaveGamble/cJSON/commit/74e1ff4994aa4139126967f6d289b675b4b36fef)). The exact parent source was cloned into an isolated target and scanned with the same bounds, integer-bounds, and unsafe-memory-call detector family, scoped to `cJSON_Utils.c`.
+
+Scan job `a3a8f05b1ce04fb99aa780f67b09860a` used commit `8f2beb57ddad1f94bed899790b00f46df893ccac`, tree `4d38dfc858efd64a1c2c4351f31676808bc0aeda`, source snapshot SHA-256 `efca85ef988ae0df7237dcdaf86a6fa5b4a8f61dfbb784000ef5373558e99a95`, and report SHA-256 `a535e90e0a0dededa1e7d030f2346dfc3785a47c36653f578350183bf2035f25`. The single C file parsed completely. Six findings were reported, all unsafe `sprintf`/`strcat` candidates at lines 234, 245, 1122, 1188, 1203, and 1248; none was at the disclosed line 285. Therefore this probe also yielded no CVE-matched candidate for the panel Agent. The source is an upstream component commit, not proof of an OpenHarmony product build or reachable product path; product status remains `unknown`. No Agent call was made on these unrelated findings.
